// cos for the back-EMF shape, sin for the Hall sensors.
from <math> import sin, cos;

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  BLDC — brushless DC machine: three-phase, trapezoidal back-EMF, Hall
//  sensors, shaft port
//
//      va ──┐                              ┌─────────────────────┐
//      vb ──┤ star, isolated neutral       │ C<J>   B·ω    T_em  │── shaft
//      vc ──┘                              └─────────────────────┘
//                hall_a, hall_b, hall_c (logic, 0/1)
//
//  The same machine as SPMSM — surface magnets, one synchronous inductance,
//  the same parameters, ports, shaft and θe — except that the magnet flux
//  is distributed so that each phase's back-EMF is trapezoidal rather than
//  sinusoidal:
//
//      v_k − v_N = Rs·i_k + Ls·di_k/dt + e_k,          k = a, b, c
//      e_k  = p·ωm·flux_m·F(θe + 90° − k·120°)
//      T_em = (e_a·i_a + e_b·i_b + e_c·i_c)/ωm = p·flux_m·Σ F_k·i_k
//
//  Ls is the per-phase inductance L − M (self minus mutual) and v_N the
//  isolated star point. θe has PMSM's meaning — the electrical angle of the
//  magnet's d axis ahead of phase a, θe = θ0 + ∫p·ωm dt — so the EMF of
//  phase a is centred on the q axis, 90° ahead of the magnet, exactly where
//  a PMSM's sinusoidal EMF −ωe·flux_m·sin θe has its positive peak.
//
//  The shape function. The ideal trapezoid is flat (±1) for 120° and ramps
//  linearly over 60°, and its corners are kinks the Newton Jacobian would
//  step across. It is replaced by
//
//      F(x) = S(2·cos x) / S(2),     S(u) = u / (1 + u^16)^(1/16)
//
//  2·cos x passes ±1 exactly 60° either side of the peak, so the plateau is
//  the ideal 120° wide; S is a smooth unit clamp (S(u) ≈ u for |u| < 0.8,
//  ≈ ±1 for |u| > 1.3), so F is infinitely differentiable. Against the ideal
//  trapezoid: F = 0.958 at the plateau corners (ideal 1), flat to 1e-4 for
//  |2·cos x| ≥ 1.5 (the middle 83° of each plateau), and the ramp is a
//  slightly curved 2·cos x instead of a straight line; zero crossings are
//  exact. flux_m is the plateau value: the flat-top phase EMF is
//  p·ωm·flux_m, i.e. Ke (V·s/rad, peak phase) = p·flux_m, and the
//  line-to-line EMF across two conducting phases is 2·p·ωm·flux_m.
//
//  The zero-sequence part of the trapezoid (its triplen harmonics) drives no
//  current through an isolated neutral and makes no torque; it only moves
//  v_N. The model is written, as PMSM, in the αβ frame (the inductor
//  currents are iα, iβ; Rs is written into the voltage sources so Rs = 0 is
//  allowed), which drops that part exactly — the phase currents, the torque,
//  and the voltage of an unenergised (floating) phase all come out as in
//  the abc equations above.
//
//  Six-step (120°) commutation. With the torque ripple-free ideal, the phase
//  conducting positive current is the one on its positive plateau and the
//  negative one the one on its negative plateau. Locked in a sector with
//  i_x = −i_y = i the torque is 2·p·flux_m·i, and at no load on a bus Vdc
//  the speed is ωm = Vdc / (2·p·flux_m) (less the switch and resistive
//  drops). The Hall sensors are placed so that their edges are the
//  commutation instants:
//
//      hall_a = 1  while  sin(θe + 150°) > 0
//      hall_b = 1  while  sin(θe +  30°) > 0
//      hall_c = 1  while  sin(θe −  90°) > 0
//
//      θe (deg)      hall a b c    drive (+ high side, − low side)
//      −150 … −90      1 0 1          a+  b−
//       −90 … −30      1 0 0          a+  c−
//       −30 …  30      1 1 0          b+  c−
//        30 …  90      0 1 0          b+  a−
//        90 … 150      0 1 1          c+  a−
//       150 … 210      0 0 1          c+  b−
//
//  which is the usual Hall sequence 101 → 100 → 110 → 010 → 011 → 001 for
//  positive rotation. Reverse rotation: swap + and − in the table. The Hall
//  outputs are logic values computed from the solved angle; a digital
//  commutation module reads them at the start of each step (sample and hold),
//  and `.zcd;` ends a step on each Hall edge.
//
//  Terminals and shaft: as PMSM — each terminal a current source to ground
//  (no phase completely open: a floating phase needs its switches' Roff or
//  diodes, as any real bridge has), V(shaft) = ωm, current = torque, J and B
//  inside, loads from shaft to ground. Run under a Newton solver.
//
//  Parameters
//      Rs      phase resistance, Ohm (0 allowed); half the line-to-line value
//      Ls      phase inductance L − M, H (> 0); half the line-to-line value
//      flux_m  plateau flux constant, Wb: flat-top phase EMF = p·ωm·flux_m.
//              From a line-to-line constant Ke_LL (V per rad/s, flat top):
//              flux_m = Ke_LL/(2·p). From a torque constant Kt (N·m/A,
//              six-step, two phases conducting): flux_m = Kt/(2·p).
//      p       number of pole PAIRS
//      J       rotor inertia, kg·m² (0 allowed — see pmsm.hvr)
//      B       rotor viscous friction, N·m·s/rad (0 allowed)
//      theta0  initial electrical rotor angle θe(0), rad
//
//  Outputs (logic)
//      omega_m_out  mechanical speed ωm, rad/s
//      hall_a_out, hall_b_out, hall_c_out   Hall states, 0.0 or 1.0
//      theta_e_out  electrical angle θe, rad (unwrapped)
//      torque_out   electromagnetic torque T_em, N·m (before friction)
//
//  Not modelled: the rounding of a real machine's EMF beyond the smooth
//  corners above (fit flux_m to the plateau), cogging torque, saturation,
//  iron loss, Hall sensor misalignment and delay, any zero-sequence path.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Smooth trapezoid, peak 1 with a 120° plateau centred on x = 0.
func double bldc_trapezoid(double x) {
    double u  = 2.0 * cos(x);
    double s  = u / ((1.0 + u**16.0)**(1.0 / 16.0));
    double s2 = 2.0 / ((1.0 + 2.0**16.0)**(1.0 / 16.0));
    return s / s2;
}

analog module BLDC<double Rs, double Ls, double flux_m, double p,
                   double J, double B, double theta0>
    (output double omega_m_out, output double hall_a_out,
     output double hall_b_out, output double hall_c_out,
     output double theta_e_out, output double torque_out)
    [wire va, wire vb, wire vc, wire shaft]
{
    wire x1, y1;

    // ── Mechanics, on the shaft node (as PMSM) ──────────────────────────────
    C<J>()                   [shaft, gnd];
    VCCS<B>()                [shaft, gnd, shaft, gnd];

    double omega_m = V(shaft);
    double omega_e = p * omega_m;

    // ── Electrical angle: θe = θ0 + ∫ p·ωm dt ───────────────────────────────
    double theta_e = theta0 + idt(omega_e);

    // ── Back-EMF shape per phase, then αβ (full Clarke: the shape has a
    //    zero-sequence part, which is dropped) ──────────────────────────────
    double f_a = bldc_trapezoid(theta_e + 1.57079632679490);
    double f_b = bldc_trapezoid(theta_e + 1.57079632679490 - 2.09439510239320);
    double f_c = bldc_trapezoid(theta_e + 1.57079632679490 + 2.09439510239320);
    double f_alpha = (2.0 * f_a - f_b - f_c) / 3.0;
    double f_beta  = (f_b - f_c) / (3.0**0.5);

    // ── Terminal voltages → αβ (amplitude-invariant, as PMSM) ───────────────
    double v_n = (V(va) + V(vb) + V(vc)) / 3.0;
    double van = V(va) - v_n;
    double vbn = V(vb) - v_n;
    double v_alpha = van;
    double v_beta  = (van + 2.0 * vbn) / (3.0**0.5);

    // ── αβ windings ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    double i_alpha = I(La_coil);
    double i_beta  = I(Lb_coil);

    double e_alpha = omega_e * flux_m * f_alpha;
    double e_beta  = omega_e * flux_m * f_beta;

    double v_La = v_alpha - Rs * i_alpha - e_alpha;
    double v_Lb = v_beta  - Rs * i_beta  - e_beta;
    voltage_source<>(v_La) [x1, gnd];
    L La_coil<Ls>()        [x1, gnd];
    voltage_source<>(v_Lb) [y1, gnd];
    L Lb_coil<Ls>()        [y1, gnd];

    // ── αβ currents → terminals ─────────────────────────────────────────────
    double i_a = i_alpha;
    double i_b = -0.5 * i_alpha + ((3.0**0.5) / 2.0) * i_beta;
    double i_c = -0.5 * i_alpha - ((3.0**0.5) / 2.0) * i_beta;

    // Motor convention: positive current flows into the terminal.
    current_source<>(i_a) [va, gnd];
    current_source<>(i_b) [vb, gnd];
    current_source<>(i_c) [vc, gnd];

    // ── Electromagnetic torque, into the shaft ──────────────────────────────
    // Σ e_k·i_k / ωm; with Σ i_k = 0 that is 1.5·(fα·iα + fβ·iβ)·p·flux_m.
    double torque = 1.5 * p * flux_m * (f_alpha * i_alpha + f_beta * i_beta);
    current_source<>(torque) [gnd, shaft];

    // ── Hall sensors: edges on the commutation instants (table above) ──────
    double h_a = 0.0;
    double h_b = 0.0;
    double h_c = 0.0;
    if (sin(theta_e + 2.61799387799149) > 0.0) { h_a = 1.0; }
    if (sin(theta_e + 0.52359877559830) > 0.0) { h_b = 1.0; }
    if (sin(theta_e - 1.57079632679490) > 0.0) { h_c = 1.0; }

    omega_m_out = omega_m;
    hall_a_out  = h_a;
    hall_b_out  = h_b;
    hall_c_out  = h_c;
    theta_e_out = theta_e;
    torque_out  = torque;
}
